\ core::prelude -- the hub. It declares no words of its own; it re-exports the
\ typed core the compiler used to inject into every program, so a consumer
\ writes one `import: core::prelude * ;` for the whole surface instead of one
\ line per module. Nothing is implicit: a program that wants none of this
\ imports none of it.
\
\ Two of `core::bool`'s names deliberately do not appear here, because a
\ re-export cannot carry them: the *type* `Bool` (a type name resolves against
\ its declaring module, so it does not follow a hub the way a word does) and
\ the bool `.` overload (an operator overload's candidate lookup considers the
\ calling module and the module it selectively imported the name from, one hop,
\ so a hub in between hides the declaring module). A program that spells `Bool`
\ in an effect or prints one imports `core::bool` directly; everything else,
\ including the `True`/`False` constructors, comes through here.
import: self::bool b | if unless False True | ;
import: self::cmp c | eq lt gt lte gte ne | ;

export: if unless False True eq lt gt lte gte ne ;
